<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oroonoko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oroonoko, or The Royal Slave, is a short novel by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphra-behn">Aphra Behn</a> published in 1688. It tells of an African prince who is tricked into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and taken to the English colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, where he leads a revolt of enslaved people and is executed. The narrator claims to have witnessed the events, and the work is one of the earliest English novels and an early literary treatment of slavery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oroonoko.jpg" alt="Oroonoko" width="221" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Behn_Oroonoko_title_page.1688.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The story sits inside a narrative frame. A woman narrator who has come to Surinam with her father, appointed lieutenant-general of the colony but dead of illness on the voyage, describes the colony and its peoples and tells the story of Oroonoko, grandson of the king of Coramantien. Trained for war, he becomes a general when the old general dies taking an arrow for him, and he falls in love with the general's daughter, Imoinda. The king takes her into his harem, sells her into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> when he learns of the lovers and tells Oroonoko she has been executed.</p><p>In Surinam, where he is renamed Caesar and she Clemene, the two meet again on the same plantation and marry. Imoinda's pregnancy drives their revolt, in which she wounds one of the masters who pursue them. Oroonoko kills her and is later executed by his owners, keeping his royal dignity to the end. The narrator, who cannot bear to watch his death, leaves Surinam for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphra-behn">Aphra Behn</a> was a successful playwright, poet and translator who turned to prose fiction in the 1680s, probably because the merging of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> reduced the demand for new plays. The book was published by William Canning less than a year before her death and reprinted that year in Three Histories. According to Charles Gildon she wrote it even with company present, and her own account suggests a single sitting.</p><p>Scholars have argued for over a century whether she ever visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>: the narrator reports sheep there, which could not survive in the colony, and Ernest Bernbaum argued that everything substantive could have come from accounts by William Byam and George Warren, while others point to her precise local detail. The settler John Allin, who plotted to kill Lord Willoughby and himself, matches aspects of the hero.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book sold, but was no great success in Behn's lifetime. Thomas Southerne's tragedy Oroonoko, staged in 1695 and published in 1696, made the story popular, and the novel was never out of print in the 18th century. Southerne made Imoinda white and added a comic subplot, later cut from performances. Critics have doubted the claim that it was the first English novel, since Behn's Love-Letters Between a Nobleman and His Sister came earlier, but it is an early novel with a linear plot on a biographical model. Behn's biographer Janet Todd holds that she did not oppose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as such; interest in the book has grown since the 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oroonoko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writer Aphra Behn claimed to have lived there and set her novel <b>Oroonoko</b> in the colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/willoughbyland">Willoughbyland</a></li></ul></section>
